{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Types of Play.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Types of Play.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"types of play types of play","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Types of Play.md","fileName":"Types of Play.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Types of Play","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 14:36\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Player Experience Design]] [[Play Styles and Structures]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Player Experience Design","Play Styles and Structures","Game Genre","advancedgamedesign.pdf","gamesdesignandplay.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Types of Play.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Types of Play.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"types of play types of play","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Types of Play.md","fileName":"Types of Play.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Types of Play","heading":"Types of Play","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Caillois's framework describes four recurring forms of play: agon centers on competition, alea on chance, mimicry on role-playing, and ilinx on altered perception or vertigo. A game may combine several rather than belonging exclusively to one.\n\nThe framework also places play on a continuum from ludus, which is structured by formal rules, to paidia, which is spontaneous and improvisational. These dimensions help describe the experience more precisely than a broad [[Game Genre]] label alone.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Player Experience Design","Play Styles and Structures","Game Genre","advancedgamedesign.pdf","gamesdesignandplay.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Types of Play.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Types of Play.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchNam